WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.890
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello, my
name is Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.330
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.850
And I'm here with
Weston Hutchins,

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.160
who's also a product
manager working on GKE.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.750
Everyone loves a
good origin story.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.250
Wes, you've been with GKE
On-Prem since the beginning.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.440
What is the origin
story of GKE On-Prem?

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.070
WESTON HUTCHINS: So, thanks
for having me here, Dustin.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.130
You know, GKE On-Prem
really started

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.810
by talking to GKE customers.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.480
So these were people
that had decided

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.758
they were using Kubernetes.

00:00:28.758 --> 00:00:31.050
And they were loving the
manage experience we give them

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.852
in the cloud, but
they needed a solution

00:00:33.852 --> 00:00:35.310
that runs in their
own data center,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.770
for a variety of reasons,
mainly regulatory.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.470
Some of them had workloads
that they couldn't really

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.290
bring up to the cloud
quite yet, legacy things.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.050
And they were looking
for a solution

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.030
that worked in both places.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.120
They had been using primarily
do-it-yourself Kubernetes,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.680
which is pretty easy
to get up and running.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.550
But the real challenge comes
on the day-two operations.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.250
So things like
securing your cluster,

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.590
doing it in high
availability mode,

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.870
just being able to scale
up the cluster up and down,

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.960
these are all things that
GKE does really well.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.270
And we talked to a lot of them.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.310
And I went and talked
to a couple of devs

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.130
inside and said, hey,
is it possible to take

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.770
the GKE management stack, all
the cluster management that we

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.470
do and a lot of
the node features,

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.480
wrap that up into a package
and bring it into a customer's

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.010
On-Prem environment?

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.290
And at first, people were
like, oh, I don't know.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.450
But over time, we
started to pull together

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.040
prototypes and pieces.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.280
And we built a package
that we could give off

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.140
to customers that was
then connected back

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.310
to our hosted control plane
that offers a local Kubernetes

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.372
cluster running
in a data center.

00:01:40.372 --> 00:01:44.580
And you know, when we talked
a lot about Anthos at Next

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.140
this year, Kubernetes
and GKE On-Prem

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.310
really formed the
base of that package.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.190
And we then start to layer
other types of services

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.800
and, I will say, ease of
use features for developers,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.770
like Istio and
Knative, that can run

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.590
in this portable environment
where you can run it

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.940
in other clouds, and you can
run it in an On-Prem place.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.520
So that's really how
GKE On-Prem came about.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.437
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And GKE
On-Prem is ultimately

00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:08.310
the heart of Anthos.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:08.729
WESTON HUTCHINS: It is.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.757
The Kubernetes is
the base underpinning

00:02:10.757 --> 00:02:12.090
of all of the stuff we build on.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.430
It gives us that
portable environment,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.050
that infrastructure
abstraction, that

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.893
allows you to run the same
workloads in different places

00:02:18.893 --> 00:02:20.310
without having to
change anything.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.620
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It all
starts with the GKE On-Prem.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.070
And then the Anthos pieces
plug in around that.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.112
WESTON HUTCHINS: Correct.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:28.920
Yeah, the base
layer is Kubernetes.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.230
It is the portable
infrastructure abstraction

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.600
that allows us to run in
an on-prem environment

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.500
and in the cloud.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.990
And then the other ones
build on top of that.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.260
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: But
Kubernetes by itself

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:38.670
just isn't enough, right?

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.400
So what are the other key
pieces that plug in that

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.025
make it enough?

00:02:42.025 --> 00:02:43.400
WESTON HUTCHINS:
Yeah, Kubernetes

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.380
is extremely powerful.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.430
But for a lot of customers I
talk to, especially developers,

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.880
they don't really want all
the bells, and whistles,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.460
and knobs of Kubernetes.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.510
And this is really where
Knative and Cloud Run fit in.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:59.220
They say, you can take the
normal app development workflow

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:04.110
that you're used to-- so push
to deploy, build in the cloud,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.700
build an app, have
different versions,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.580
scale those out-- and under
the covers, that will actually

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.830
go and configure
traffic splitting

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.470
services in Kubernetes,
ad load balancers,

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.870
scale up apps, scale down apps.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.317
That's really what
Kubernetes is supposed to be.

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:19.900
It's the base layer
that gets it done,

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.300
but we want to
make things easier

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.620
for developers and operators.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.450
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And is this meeting,

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.235
those same customers
that you talked to

00:03:25.235 --> 00:03:26.610
at the beginning
that didn't want

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.515
those day-2, day-92
operations, don't

00:03:29.515 --> 00:03:31.890
want to worry about those
operations and that management,

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.560
are we hitting the mark here?

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.560
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.930
You know, for the most part,
most customers don't really

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.880
want to be in the business
of operating and managing

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:39.630
their own cluster.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.130
They would rather
let someone that

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.280
knows how to do this at scale
with many, many customers

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.952
take that over for them, so they
can just focus on writing apps.

00:03:46.952 --> 00:03:47.910
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.770
And what are you
hearing in terms

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:54.390
of hybrid workloads and the
challenges and solutions

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.273
to hybrid computing?

00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:56.190
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.850
So the biggest challenge that we
ran into is just a lot of these

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.940
customers either have data that
cannot leave the data center

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.430
for regulatory reasons, or
maybe they need to run workloads

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.700
at the edge, so super fast,
low latency processing.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.680
They have standardized
on Kubernetes.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.840
They said, that is
the app platform

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.750
that we want to use in the
cloud and in these environments,

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.209
but we really need
a full managed stack

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.350
running in these places.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.740
And so GKE On-Prem does
give them that ability

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.400
to keep data local, to
work within their existing

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.330
regulatory environments, but
also offers them a path forward

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.050
to cloud.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.350
This is the real big,
key benefit of Anthos

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.790
is that you can start to
use cloud workflows, right?

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.070
You can start to
think about writing

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.890
apps that are
resilient to clusters,

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.250
nodes that can go down.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.340
You can start thinking
about global apps

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.600
and how to do traffic
splitting across them.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.360
But you can use that in your own
environment when you're ready.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.910
You can move up to the
cloud at your own pace.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:55.260
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It's
a real hybrid world.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:55.650
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:57.025
For a lot of
customers, it really

00:04:57.025 --> 00:05:01.030
is about training and
the people that you

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.920
hire, training them
on the right tools,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.470
so that they don't have to
learn different types of tooling

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:06.845
for these different
environments.

00:05:06.845 --> 00:05:08.850
That's a lot of the magic
of what Anthos brings.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.600
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Wes,
thank you very much.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.110
WESTON HUTCHINS: Absolutely.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:12.190
Thank you.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.240
[SOFT MUSIC]

